              "fullDescription": {
                "markdown": "### Impact\n\nA single request can crash any application using `body-parser` with default options. When a request carries a `Content-Encoding` (for example `gzip`), `body-parser` pipes it into a decompression stream and, after reading the body, leaves that stream piped with no error listener. A payload of one valid compressed frame followed by trailing bytes decodes and the application responds normally, but the trailing bytes then reach the already-finished decoder, which emits an error that is re-thrown as an uncaught exception and terminates the process. No authentication or matching route is required, since parsing runs before routing.\n\n### Patches\n\nFixed in body-parser 2.4.0 and 1.20.9. Upgrade to one of these versions.\n\n### Workarounds\n\nPass `inflate: false` to the body-parser middleware if the application does not need to accept compressed request bodies. This rejects encoded requests before any decompression stream is created.",
                "text": "### Impact\n\nA single request can crash any application using `body-parser` with default options. When a request carries a `Content-Encoding` (for example `gzip`), `body-parser` pipes it into a decompression stream and, after reading the body, leaves that stream piped with no error listener. A payload of one valid compressed frame followed by trailing bytes decodes and the application responds normally, but the trailing bytes then reach the already-finished decoder, which emits an error that is re-thrown as an uncaught exception and terminates the process. No authentication or matching route is required, since parsing runs before routing.\n\n### Patches\n\nFixed in body-parser 2.4.0 and 1.20.9. Upgrade to one of these versions.\n\n### Workarounds\n\nPass `inflate: false` to the body-parser middleware if the application does not need to accept compressed request bodies. This rejects encoded requests before any decompression stream is created."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [CVE-2026-111181](https://osv.dev/CVE-2026-111181)**.\n\n## [GHSA-27r3-jxmh-p828](https://osv.dev/GHSA-27r3-jxmh-p828)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ### Impact\n\u003e \n\u003e A single request can crash any application using `body-parser` with default options. When a request carries a `Content-Encoding` (for example `gzip`), `body-parser` pipes it into a decompression stream and, after reading the body, leaves that stream piped with no error listener. A payload of one valid compressed frame followed by trailing bytes decodes and the application responds normally, but the trailing bytes then reach the already-finished decoder, which emits an error that is re-thrown as an uncaught exception and terminates the process. No authentication or matching route is required, since parsing runs before routing.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Patches\n\u003e \n\u003e Fixed in body-parser 2.4.0 and 1.20.9. Upgrade to one of these versions.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Workarounds\n\u003e \n\u003e Pass `inflate: false` to the body-parser middleware if the application does not need to accept compressed request bodies. This rejects encoded requests before any decompression stream is created.\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/src/package-lock.json | body-parser | 1.20.8 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GHSA-27r3-jxmh-p828 | body-parser | 1.20.9, 2.4.0 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them, add them to the ignore list in an\n`osv-scanner.toml` file located in the same directory as the lockfile containing the vulnerable dependency.\n\nSee the format and more options in our documentation here: https://google.github.io/osv-scanner/configuration/\n\nAdd or append these values to the following config files to ignore this vulnerability:\n\n`/src/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-111181\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n",
                "text": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [CVE-2026-111181](https://osv.dev/CVE-2026-111181)**.\n\n## [GHSA-27r3-jxmh-p828](https://osv.dev/GHSA-27r3-jxmh-p828)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ### Impact\n\u003e \n\u003e A single request can crash any application using `body-parser` with default options. When a request carries a `Content-Encoding` (for example `gzip`), `body-parser` pipes it into a decompression stream and, after reading the body, leaves that stream piped with no error listener. A payload of one valid compressed frame followed by trailing bytes decodes and the application responds normally, but the trailing bytes then reach the already-finished decoder, which emits an error that is re-thrown as an uncaught exception and terminates the process. No authentication or matching route is required, since parsing runs before routing.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Patches\n\u003e \n\u003e Fixed in body-parser 2.4.0 and 1.20.9. Upgrade to one of these versions.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Workarounds\n\u003e \n\u003e Pass `inflate: false` to the body-parser middleware if the application does not need to accept compressed request bodies. This rejects encoded requests before any decompression stream is created.\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/src/package-lock.json | body-parser | 1.20.8 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GHSA-27r3-jxmh-p828 | body-parser | 1.20.9, 2.4.0 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them, add them to the ignore list in an\n`osv-scanner.toml` file located in the same directory as the lockfile containing the vulnerable dependency.\n\nSee the format and more options in our documentation here: https://google.github.io/osv-scanner/configuration/\n\nAdd or append these values to the following config files to ignore this vulnerability:\n\n`/src/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-111181\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n"
