              "deprecatedIds": [
                "CVE-2026-118664",
                "GHSA-qr28-fpjm-wq7f"
              ],
              "fullDescription": {
                "markdown": "### Summary\n\n`decodeUriComponent()` takes quadratic time on malformed input that has many distinct percent-encoded runs. A single unauthenticated request of about 224 KB can block the Node.js event loop for about 20 seconds. Version 0.5.0, the release that fixed CVE-2026-45822, is affected.\n\n### Details\n\nWhen native decodeURIComponent throws, `decodeUriComponent()` falls back to `customDecodeURIComponent` (`index.js:122`). That function:\n1. Scans the input for every run of percent-encoded bytes (e.g. `%C3%A5`) and stores each distinct run, with its decoded value, in a lookup table (`index.js:130-144`).\n2. Runs one full-string `input.replace(new RegExp(key, 'g'), …)` per table entry (`index.js:154`).\n\nWith K distinct runs in an input of length L, that's K regex compilations and K full scans of the string, so O(K·L). An attacker controls both K and L, and K grows with L. One malformed sequence such as a trailing `%zz` is enough to force the fallback path.\n\nThe 0.5.0 fix for CVE-2026-45822 (`fa479da`) replaced the recursive `decode()` routine with a linear scanner, but left this replacement loop unchanged.\n\n### Proof of concept\n\nRunning the following code takes ~20 seconds.\n\n```js\nimport decodeUriComponent from 'decode-uri-component';\n\nlet payload = '';\nfor (let i = 0; i \u003c 32000; i++) {\n\tconst high = Math.floor(i / 256).toString(16).padStart(2, '0');\n\tconst low = (i % 256).toString(16).padStart(2, '0');\n\tpayload += `%${high}%${low}x`; // distinct run each iteration\n}\npayload += '%zz'; // forces the fallback decoder\n\nconsole.time('decode');\ndecodeUriComponent(payload); // ~224 KB\nconsole.timeEnd('decode');\n```\n\n### Impact\n\nThis is an availability issue only and there's no confidentiality or integrity impact. Any application that passes untrusted input to `decodeUriComponent()`, directly or through a dependency such as `query-string`, can be made unresponsive. Because Node.js runs on one thread, one request stalls every other client.\n\nThe worst case needs input well beyond URL length limits. Node's default 16 KB header limit keeps payloads passed in via a URL query string to roughly 40 ms per request. Applications that decode request bodies are fully exposed, for example form bodies parsed with `query-string.parse()`.",
                "text": "### Summary\n\n`decodeUriComponent()` takes quadratic time on malformed input that has many distinct percent-encoded runs. A single unauthenticated request of about 224 KB can block the Node.js event loop for about 20 seconds. Version 0.5.0, the release that fixed CVE-2026-45822, is affected.\n\n### Details\n\nWhen native decodeURIComponent throws, `decodeUriComponent()` falls back to `customDecodeURIComponent` (`index.js:122`). That function:\n1. Scans the input for every run of percent-encoded bytes (e.g. `%C3%A5`) and stores each distinct run, with its decoded value, in a lookup table (`index.js:130-144`).\n2. Runs one full-string `input.replace(new RegExp(key, 'g'), …)` per table entry (`index.js:154`).\n\nWith K distinct runs in an input of length L, that's K regex compilations and K full scans of the string, so O(K·L). An attacker controls both K and L, and K grows with L. One malformed sequence such as a trailing `%zz` is enough to force the fallback path.\n\nThe 0.5.0 fix for CVE-2026-45822 (`fa479da`) replaced the recursive `decode()` routine with a linear scanner, but left this replacement loop unchanged.\n\n### Proof of concept\n\nRunning the following code takes ~20 seconds.\n\n```js\nimport decodeUriComponent from 'decode-uri-component';\n\nlet payload = '';\nfor (let i = 0; i \u003c 32000; i++) {\n\tconst high = Math.floor(i / 256).toString(16).padStart(2, '0');\n\tconst low = (i % 256).toString(16).padStart(2, '0');\n\tpayload += `%${high}%${low}x`; // distinct run each iteration\n}\npayload += '%zz'; // forces the fallback decoder\n\nconsole.time('decode');\ndecodeUriComponent(payload); // ~224 KB\nconsole.timeEnd('decode');\n```\n\n### Impact\n\nThis is an availability issue only and there's no confidentiality or integrity impact. Any application that passes untrusted input to `decodeUriComponent()`, directly or through a dependency such as `query-string`, can be made unresponsive. Because Node.js runs on one thread, one request stalls every other client.\n\nThe worst case needs input well beyond URL length limits. Node's default 16 KB header limit keeps payloads passed in via a URL query string to roughly 40 ms per request. Applications that decode request bodies are fully exposed, for example form bodies parsed with `query-string.parse()`."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [CVE-2026-118664](https://osv.dev/CVE-2026-118664)**.\n\n## [GHSA-qr28-fpjm-wq7f](https://osv.dev/GHSA-qr28-fpjm-wq7f)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ### Summary\n\u003e \n\u003e `decodeUriComponent()` takes quadratic time on malformed input that has many distinct percent-encoded runs. A single unauthenticated request of about 224 KB can block the Node.js event loop for about 20 seconds. Version 0.5.0, the release that fixed CVE-2026-45822, is affected.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Details\n\u003e \n\u003e When native decodeURIComponent throws, `decodeUriComponent()` falls back to `customDecodeURIComponent` (`index.js:122`). That function:\n\u003e 1. Scans the input for every run of percent-encoded bytes (e.g. `%C3%A5`) and stores each distinct run, with its decoded value, in a lookup table (`index.js:130-144`).\n\u003e 2. Runs one full-string `input.replace(new RegExp(key, 'g'), …)` per table entry (`index.js:154`).\n\u003e \n\u003e With K distinct runs in an input of length L, that's K regex compilations and K full scans of the string, so O(K·L). An attacker controls both K and L, and K grows with L. One malformed sequence such as a trailing `%zz` is enough to force the fallback path.\n\u003e \n\u003e The 0.5.0 fix for CVE-2026-45822 (`fa479da`) replaced the recursive `decode()` routine with a linear scanner, but left this replacement loop unchanged.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Proof of concept\n\u003e \n\u003e Running the following code takes ~20 seconds.\n\u003e \n\u003e ```js\n\u003e import decodeUriComponent from 'decode-uri-component';\n\u003e \n\u003e let payload = '';\n\u003e for (let i = 0; i \u003c 32000; i++) {\n\u003e \tconst high = Math.floor(i / 256).toString(16).padStart(2, '0');\n\u003e \tconst low = (i % 256).toString(16).padStart(2, '0');\n\u003e \tpayload += `%${high}%${low}x`; // distinct run each iteration\n\u003e }\n\u003e payload += '%zz'; // forces the fallback decoder\n\u003e \n\u003e console.time('decode');\n\u003e decodeUriComponent(payload); // ~224 KB\n\u003e console.timeEnd('decode');\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Impact\n\u003e \n\u003e This is an availability issue only and there's no confidentiality or integrity impact. Any application that passes untrusted input to `decodeUriComponent()`, directly or through a dependency such as `query-string`, can be made unresponsive. Because Node.js runs on one thread, one request stalls every other client.\n\u003e \n\u003e The worst case needs input well beyond URL length limits. Node's default 16 KB header limit keeps payloads passed in via a URL query string to roughly 40 ms per request. Applications that decode request bodies are fully exposed, for example form bodies parsed with `query-string.parse()`.\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/src/package-lock.json | decode-uri-component | 0.2.2 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GHSA-qr28-fpjm-wq7f | decode-uri-component | 0.5.1 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them, add them to the ignore list in an\n`osv-scanner.toml` file located in the same directory as the lockfile containing the vulnerable dependency.\n\nSee the format and more options in our documentation here: https://google.github.io/osv-scanner/configuration/\n\nAdd or append these values to the following config files to ignore this vulnerability:\n\n`/src/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-118664\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n",
                "text": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [CVE-2026-118664](https://osv.dev/CVE-2026-118664)**.\n\n## [GHSA-qr28-fpjm-wq7f](https://osv.dev/GHSA-qr28-fpjm-wq7f)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ### Summary\n\u003e \n\u003e `decodeUriComponent()` takes quadratic time on malformed input that has many distinct percent-encoded runs. A single unauthenticated request of about 224 KB can block the Node.js event loop for about 20 seconds. Version 0.5.0, the release that fixed CVE-2026-45822, is affected.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Details\n\u003e \n\u003e When native decodeURIComponent throws, `decodeUriComponent()` falls back to `customDecodeURIComponent` (`index.js:122`). That function:\n\u003e 1. Scans the input for every run of percent-encoded bytes (e.g. `%C3%A5`) and stores each distinct run, with its decoded value, in a lookup table (`index.js:130-144`).\n\u003e 2. Runs one full-string `input.replace(new RegExp(key, 'g'), …)` per table entry (`index.js:154`).\n\u003e \n\u003e With K distinct runs in an input of length L, that's K regex compilations and K full scans of the string, so O(K·L). An attacker controls both K and L, and K grows with L. One malformed sequence such as a trailing `%zz` is enough to force the fallback path.\n\u003e \n\u003e The 0.5.0 fix for CVE-2026-45822 (`fa479da`) replaced the recursive `decode()` routine with a linear scanner, but left this replacement loop unchanged.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Proof of concept\n\u003e \n\u003e Running the following code takes ~20 seconds.\n\u003e \n\u003e ```js\n\u003e import decodeUriComponent from 'decode-uri-component';\n\u003e \n\u003e let payload = '';\n\u003e for (let i = 0; i \u003c 32000; i++) {\n\u003e \tconst high = Math.floor(i / 256).toString(16).padStart(2, '0');\n\u003e \tconst low = (i % 256).toString(16).padStart(2, '0');\n\u003e \tpayload += `%${high}%${low}x`; // distinct run each iteration\n\u003e }\n\u003e payload += '%zz'; // forces