              "fullDescription": {
                "markdown": "## Summary\n\n`sanitize-html` discards the *content* of every element in `nonTextTags`. It delimits that region\nusing htmlparser2's close events, and ignores the `isImplied` flag htmlparser2 passes it. Closing a\nstill-open **ancestor** from inside a `\u003cnoscript\u003e` makes htmlparser2 unwind its stack past the\n`noscript`; the synthetic implied close it emits on the way ends the discard region early, and\neverything after it is treated as ordinary body content.\n\nA browser with scripting enabled parses `\u003cnoscript\u003e` content as **raw text**, so the same markup\nrenders as inert text there. The sanitizer therefore emits elements the page itself would not have.\n\n## Reproduction\n\n```js\nconst sanitizeHtml = require(\"sanitize-html\");\n\nsanitizeHtml('\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cnoscript\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\u003cimg src=\"http://evil.test/t.gif\"\u003e\u003c/noscript\u003e', {\n  allowedTags: [\"div\", \"img\"],\n  allowedAttributes: { img: [\"src\"] },\n  nonTextTags: [\"script\", \"style\", \"textarea\", \"option\", \"noscript\"],\n});\n// =\u003e '\u003cdiv\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\u003cimg src=\"http://evil.test/t.gif\" /\u003e'\n```\n\nChrome, given the same input, renders **no** `\u003cimg\u003e` — the whole payload is raw text inside the\nunclosed `\u003cnoscript\u003e`. Verified with Playwright, `srcdoc` into an iframe so the scripting flag is on.\n\nA `\u003c![CDATA[` variant hides the opening tag from the parser entirely and has the same effect:\n\n```\n\u003cp\u003eHi\u003c/p\u003e\u003c![CDATA[ x \u003e \u003cnoscript\u003e]]\u003e\u003ca href=\"…\"\u003eReset your password\u003c/a\u003e\u003cimg src=\"…beacon.gif\"\u003e\u003c/noscript\u003e\n```\n\nhtmlparser2 runs a CDATA token to `]]\u003e`; Chrome ends a bogus comment at the first `\u003e`, so the\n`\u003cnoscript\u003e` after it is real to the browser and invisible to the parser.\n\n## Where the behaviour is\n\n`index.js`:\n\n- `:154` — `nonTextTagsArray` (the default list, `['script','style','textarea','option','xmp']`).\n- `:638` — `onclosetag: function (name, isImplied)` — receives the flag.\n- `:644` — `skipTextDepth--` — decrements without consulting `isImplied`.\n\n## Suggested fix\n\nDo not end a discard region on an implied close. htmlparser2 already distinguishes them; the flag\nis passed and discarded. Something close to:\n\n```js\nonclosetag: function (name, isImplied) {\n  if (skipText) {\n    if (isImplied) return;   // an implied close must not end the region\n    skipTextDepth--;\n    …\n```",
                "text": "## Summary\n\n`sanitize-html` discards the *content* of every element in `nonTextTags`. It delimits that region\nusing htmlparser2's close events, and ignores the `isImplied` flag htmlparser2 passes it. Closing a\nstill-open **ancestor** from inside a `\u003cnoscript\u003e` makes htmlparser2 unwind its stack past the\n`noscript`; the synthetic implied close it emits on the way ends the discard region early, and\neverything after it is treated as ordinary body content.\n\nA browser with scripting enabled parses `\u003cnoscript\u003e` content as **raw text**, so the same markup\nrenders as inert text there. The sanitizer therefore emits elements the page itself would not have.\n\n## Reproduction\n\n```js\nconst sanitizeHtml = require(\"sanitize-html\");\n\nsanitizeHtml('\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cnoscript\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\u003cimg src=\"http://evil.test/t.gif\"\u003e\u003c/noscript\u003e', {\n  allowedTags: [\"div\", \"img\"],\n  allowedAttributes: { img: [\"src\"] },\n  nonTextTags: [\"script\", \"style\", \"textarea\", \"option\", \"noscript\"],\n});\n// =\u003e '\u003cdiv\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\u003cimg src=\"http://evil.test/t.gif\" /\u003e'\n```\n\nChrome, given the same input, renders **no** `\u003cimg\u003e` — the whole payload is raw text inside the\nunclosed `\u003cnoscript\u003e`. Verified with Playwright, `srcdoc` into an iframe so the scripting flag is on.\n\nA `\u003c![CDATA[` variant hides the opening tag from the parser entirely and has the same effect:\n\n```\n\u003cp\u003eHi\u003c/p\u003e\u003c![CDATA[ x \u003e \u003cnoscript\u003e]]\u003e\u003ca href=\"…\"\u003eReset your password\u003c/a\u003e\u003cimg src=\"…beacon.gif\"\u003e\u003c/noscript\u003e\n```\n\nhtmlparser2 runs a CDATA token to `]]\u003e`; Chrome ends a bogus comment at the first `\u003e`, so the\n`\u003cnoscript\u003e` after it is real to the browser and invisible to the parser.\n\n## Where the behaviour is\n\n`index.js`:\n\n- `:154` — `nonTextTagsArray` (the default list, `['script','style','textarea','option','xmp']`).\n- `:638` — `onclosetag: function (name, isImplied)` — receives the flag.\n- `:644` — `skipTextDepth--` — decrements without consulting `isImplied`.\n\n## Suggested fix\n\nDo not end a discard region on an implied close. htmlparser2 already distinguishes them; the flag\nis passed and discarded. Something close to:\n\n```js\nonclosetag: function (name, isImplied) {\n  if (skipText) {\n    if (isImplied) return;   // an implied close must not end the region\n    skipTextDepth--;\n    …\n```"
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [CVE-2026-113654](https://osv.dev/CVE-2026-113654)**.\n\n## [GHSA-x3q4-9hxx-gx8m](https://osv.dev/GHSA-x3q4-9hxx-gx8m)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ## Summary\n\u003e \n\u003e `sanitize-html` discards the *content* of every element in `nonTextTags`. It delimits that region\n\u003e using htmlparser2's close events, and ignores the `isImplied` flag htmlparser2 passes it. Closing a\n\u003e still-open **ancestor** from inside a `\u003cnoscript\u003e` makes htmlparser2 unwind its stack past the\n\u003e `noscript`; the synthetic implied close it emits on the way ends the discard region early, and\n\u003e everything after it is treated as ordinary body content.\n\u003e \n\u003e A browser with scripting enabled parses `\u003cnoscript\u003e` content as **raw text**, so the same markup\n\u003e renders as inert text there. The sanitizer therefore emits elements the page itself would not have.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Reproduction\n\u003e \n\u003e ```js\n\u003e const sanitizeHtml = require(\"sanitize-html\");\n\u003e \n\u003e sanitizeHtml('\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cnoscript\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\u003cimg src=\"http://evil.test/t.gif\"\u003e\u003c/noscript\u003e', {\n\u003e   allowedTags: [\"div\", \"img\"],\n\u003e   allowedAttributes: { img: [\"src\"] },\n\u003e   nonTextTags: [\"script\", \"style\", \"textarea\", \"option\", \"noscript\"],\n\u003e });\n\u003e // =\u003e '\u003cdiv\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\u003cimg src=\"http://evil.test/t.gif\" /\u003e'\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e Chrome, given the same input, renders **no** `\u003cimg\u003e` — the whole payload is raw text inside the\n\u003e unclosed `\u003cnoscript\u003e`. Verified with Playwright, `srcdoc` into an iframe so the scripting flag is on.\n\u003e \n\u003e A `\u003c![CDATA[` variant hides the opening tag from the parser entirely and has the same effect:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```\n\u003e \u003cp\u003eHi\u003c/p\u003e\u003c![CDATA[ x \u003e \u003cnoscript\u003e]]\u003e\u003ca href=\"…\"\u003eReset your password\u003c/a\u003e\u003cimg src=\"…beacon.gif\"\u003e\u003c/noscript\u003e\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e htmlparser2 runs a CDATA token to `]]\u003e`; Chrome ends a bogus comment at the first `\u003e`, so the\n\u003e `\u003cnoscript\u003e` after it is real to the browser and invisible to the parser.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Where the behaviour is\n\u003e \n\u003e `index.js`:\n\u003e \n\u003e - `:154` — `nonTextTagsArray` (the default list, `['script','style','textarea','option','xmp']`).\n\u003e - `:638` — `onclosetag: function (name, isImplied)` — receives the flag.\n\u003e - `:644` — `skipTextDepth--` — decrements without consulting `isImplied`.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Suggested fix\n\u003e \n\u003e Do not end a discard region on an implied close. htmlparser2 already distinguishes them; the flag\n\u003e is passed and discarded. Something close to:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```js\n\u003e onclosetag: function (name, isImplied) {\n\u003e   if (skipText) {\n\u003e     if (isImplied) return;   // an implied close must not end the region\n\u003e     skipTextDepth--;\n\u003e     …\n\u003e ```\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/src/package-lock.json | sanitize-html | 1.4.2 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GHSA-x3q4-9hxx-gx8m | sanitize-html | 2.18.0 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them, add them to the ignore list in an\n`osv-scanner.toml` file located in the same directory as the lockfile containing the vulnerable dependency.\n\nSee the format and more options in our documentation here: https://google.github.io/osv-scanner/configuration/\n\nAdd or append these values to the following config files to ignore this vulnerability:\n\n`/src/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-113654\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n",
                "text": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [CVE-2026-113654](https://osv.dev/CVE-2026-113654)**.\n\n## [GHSA-x3q4-9hxx-gx8m](https://osv.dev/GHSA-x3q4-9hxx-gx8m)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ## Summary\n\u003e \n\u003e `sanitize-html` discards the *content* of every element in `nonTextTags`. It delimits that region\n\u003e using htmlparser2's close events, and ignores the `isImplied` flag htmlparser2 passes it. Closing a\n\u003e still-open **ancestor** from inside a `\u003cnoscript\u003e` makes htmlparser2 unwind its stack past the\n\u003e `noscript`; the synthetic implied close it emits on the way ends the discard region early, and\n\u003e everything after it is treated as ordinary body content.\n\u003e \n\u003e A browser with scripting enabled parses `\u003cnoscript\u003e` content as **raw text**, so the same markup\n\u003e renders as inert text there. The sanitizer therefore emits elements the page itself would not have.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Reproduction\n\u003e \n\u003e ```js\n\u003e const sanitizeHtml = require(\"sanitize-html\");\n\u003e \n\u003e sanitizeHtml('\u003cdiv\u003e\u003cnoscript\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\u003cimg src=\"http://evil.test/t.gif\"\u003e\u003c/noscript\u003e', {\n\u003e   allowedTags: [\"div\", \"img\"],\n\u003e   allowedAttributes: { img: [\"src\"] },\n\u003e   nonTextTags: [\"script\", \"style\", \"textarea\", \"option\", \"noscript\"],\n\u003e });\n\u003e // =\u003e '\u003cdiv\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\u003cimg src=\"http://evil.test/t.gif\" /\u003e'\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e Chrome, given the same input, renders **no** `\u003cimg\u003e` — the whole payload is raw text inside the\n\u003e unclosed `\u003cnoscript\u003e`. Verified with Playwright, `srcdoc` into an iframe so the scripting flag is on.\n\u003e \n\u003e A `\u003c![CDATA[` variant hides the opening tag from the parser entirely and has the same effect:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```\n\u003e \u003cp\u003eHi\u003c/p\u003e\u003c![CDATA[ x \u003e \u003cnoscript\u003e]]\u003e\u003ca href=\"…\"\u003eReset your password\u003c/a\u003e\u003cimg src=\"…beacon.gif\"\u003e\u003c/noscript\u003e\n\u003e ```\n\u003e \n\u003e htmlparser2 runs a CDATA token to `]]\u003e`; Chrome ends a bogus comment at the first `\u003e`, so the\n\u003e `\u003cnoscript\u003e` after it is real to the browser and invisible to the parser.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Where the behaviour is\n\u003e \n\u003e `index.js`:\n\u003e \n\u003e - `:154` — `nonTextTagsArray` (the default list, `['script','style','textarea','option','xmp']`).\n\u003e - `:638` — `onclosetag: function (name, isImplied)` — receives the flag.\n\u003e - `:644` — `skipTextDepth--` — decrements without consulting `isImplied`.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## Suggested fix\n\u003e \n\u003e Do not end a discard region on an implied close. htmlparser2 already distinguishes them; the flag\n\u003e is passed and discarded. Something close to:\n\u003e \n\u003e ```js\n\u003e onclosetag: function (name, isImplied) {\n\u003e   if (skipText) {\n\u003e     if (isImplied) return;   // an implied close must not end the region\n\u003e     skipTextDepth--;\n\u003e     …\n\u003e ```\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/src/package-lock.json | sanitize-html | 1.4.2 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GHSA-x3q4-9hxx-gx8m | sanitize-html | 2.18.0 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them, add them to the ignore list in an\n`osv-scanner.toml` file located in the same directory as the lockfile containing the vulnerable dependency.\n\nSee the format and more options in our documentation here: https://google.github.io/osv-scanner/configuration/\n\nAdd or append these values to