            {
              "deprecatedIds": [
                "CVE-2026-110095",
                "GHSA-xhq7-xmqj-769h"
              ],
              "fullDescription": {
                "markdown": "### Impact\n\n`fast-uri`'s `normalize()` and `serialize()` apply RFC 3986 dot-segment removal (`remove_dot_segments`) to the path of a relative-path reference. RFC 3986 section 5.2.4 defines that algorithm only for the merged target path during resolution, not for the reference itself, so applied to the reference it drops leading `..` segments and can turn a document-relative reference into a root-relative one.\n\nFor example, `normalize('a/../b')` returns `/b` and `normalize('../x')` returns `x`, so `resolve(base, normalize(ref))` points at a different resource than `resolve(base, ref)`, and `equal()` treats distinct references as the same.\n\nApplications that normalize attacker-supplied or user-supplied relative references and then resolve them, or use them as cache, deduplication, or authorization keys, can have a reference escape its intended directory and point at a different resource.\n\n### Patches\n\nUpgrade to `fast-uri` `2.4.8`, `3.1.9`, or `4.2.3`.\n\n### Workarounds\n\nDo not normalize a relative reference before resolving it. Resolve it against the absolute base first, then normalize the absolute result.",
                "text": "### Impact\n\n`fast-uri`'s `normalize()` and `serialize()` apply RFC 3986 dot-segment removal (`remove_dot_segments`) to the path of a relative-path reference. RFC 3986 section 5.2.4 defines that algorithm only for the merged target path during resolution, not for the reference itself, so applied to the reference it drops leading `..` segments and can turn a document-relative reference into a root-relative one.\n\nFor example, `normalize('a/../b')` returns `/b` and `normalize('../x')` returns `x`, so `resolve(base, normalize(ref))` points at a different resource than `resolve(base, ref)`, and `equal()` treats distinct references as the same.\n\nApplications that normalize attacker-supplied or user-supplied relative references and then resolve them, or use them as cache, deduplication, or authorization keys, can have a reference escape its intended directory and point at a different resource.\n\n### Patches\n\nUpgrade to `fast-uri` `2.4.8`, `3.1.9`, or `4.2.3`.\n\n### Workarounds\n\nDo not normalize a relative reference before resolving it. Resolve it against the absolute base first, then normalize the absolute result."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [CVE-2026-110095](https://osv.dev/CVE-2026-110095)**.\n\n## [GHSA-xhq7-xmqj-769h](https://osv.dev/GHSA-xhq7-xmqj-769h)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ### Impact\n\u003e \n\u003e `fast-uri`'s `normalize()` and `serialize()` apply RFC 3986 dot-segment removal (`remove_dot_segments`) to the path of a relative-path reference. RFC 3986 section 5.2.4 defines that algorithm only for the merged target path during resolution, not for the reference itself, so applied to the reference it drops leading `..` segments and can turn a document-relative reference into a root-relative one.\n\u003e \n\u003e For example, `normalize('a/../b')` returns `/b` and `normalize('../x')` returns `x`, so `resolve(base, normalize(ref))` points at a different resource than `resolve(base, ref)`, and `equal()` treats distinct references as the same.\n\u003e \n\u003e Applications that normalize attacker-supplied or user-supplied relative references and then resolve them, or use them as cache, deduplication, or authorization keys, can have a reference escape its intended directory and point at a different resource.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Patches\n\u003e \n\u003e Upgrade to `fast-uri` `2.4.8`, `3.1.9`, or `4.2.3`.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Workarounds\n\u003e \n\u003e Do not normalize a relative reference before resolving it. Resolve it against the absolute base first, then normalize the absolute result.\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/src/package-lock.json | fast-uri | 3.1.8 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GHSA-xhq7-xmqj-769h | fast-uri | 2.4.8, 3.1.9, 4.2.3 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them, add them to the ignore list in an\n`osv-scanner.toml` file located in the same directory as the lockfile containing the vulnerable dependency.\n\nSee the format and more options in our documentation here: https://google.github.io/osv-scanner/configuration/\n\nAdd or append these values to the following config files to ignore this vulnerability:\n\n`/src/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-110095\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n",
                "text": "**Your dependency is vulnerable to [CVE-2026-110095](https://osv.dev/CVE-2026-110095)**.\n\n## [GHSA-xhq7-xmqj-769h](https://osv.dev/GHSA-xhq7-xmqj-769h)\n\n\u003cdetails\u003e\n\u003csummary\u003eDetails\u003c/summary\u003e\n\n\u003e ### Impact\n\u003e \n\u003e `fast-uri`'s `normalize()` and `serialize()` apply RFC 3986 dot-segment removal (`remove_dot_segments`) to the path of a relative-path reference. RFC 3986 section 5.2.4 defines that algorithm only for the merged target path during resolution, not for the reference itself, so applied to the reference it drops leading `..` segments and can turn a document-relative reference into a root-relative one.\n\u003e \n\u003e For example, `normalize('a/../b')` returns `/b` and `normalize('../x')` returns `x`, so `resolve(base, normalize(ref))` points at a different resource than `resolve(base, ref)`, and `equal()` treats distinct references as the same.\n\u003e \n\u003e Applications that normalize attacker-supplied or user-supplied relative references and then resolve them, or use them as cache, deduplication, or authorization keys, can have a reference escape its intended directory and point at a different resource.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Patches\n\u003e \n\u003e Upgrade to `fast-uri` `2.4.8`, `3.1.9`, or `4.2.3`.\n\u003e \n\u003e ### Workarounds\n\u003e \n\u003e Do not normalize a relative reference before resolving it. Resolve it against the absolute base first, then normalize the absolute result.\n\n\u003c/details\u003e\n\n---\n\n### Affected Packages\n\n| Source | Package Name | Package Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| lockfile:/src/package-lock.json | fast-uri | 3.1.8 |\n\n## Remediation\n\nTo fix these vulnerabilities, update the vulnerabilities past the listed fixed versions below.\n\n### Fixed Versions\n\n| Vulnerability ID | Package Name | Fixed Version |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| GHSA-xhq7-xmqj-769h | fast-uri | 2.4.8, 3.1.9, 4.2.3 |\n\nIf you believe these vulnerabilities do not affect your code and wish to ignore them, add them to the ignore list in an\n`osv-scanner.toml` file located in the same directory as the lockfile containing the vulnerable dependency.\n\nSee the format and more options in our documentation here: https://google.github.io/osv-scanner/configuration/\n\nAdd or append these values to the following config files to ignore this vulnerability:\n\n`/src/osv-scanner.toml`\n\n```\n[[IgnoredVulns]]\nid = \"CVE-2026-110095\"\nreason = \"Your reason for ignoring this vulnerability\"\n```\n"
              },
